      "title": "HTTP/3: body reassembly and dissection",
      "message": "HTTP/3: body reassembly and dissection\n\nBody reassembly. Use Content-Length when availalbe, QUIC FIN when\nnote. Note that HTTP/3 relies on QUIC FIN and doesn't have a flags\nfield with END_STREAM. That presents some trickiness, because multiple\nHTTP/3 frames -- or none at all -- can be contained in the QUIC\nSTREAM frame that has the FIN.\n\nBody decompression. Preference for decompression. Expert info and\nrefusal to send to content-type dissector if decompression is disabled\nor failed (or for partial content 206 responses). Send information\nto follow stream after decompression.\n\nBody dissection. Call the media type subdissector for the content-type,\nif there's an entry in the media type table.\n\nAdd an expert info for Transfer Encoding, which MUST NOT be used on\nHTTP/3.\n\nFuture enhancements to consider, not yet implemented:\nStreaming dissection mode a la HTTP/2 for subdissectors that support\nthat.\nExport Objects\nPre-parsing some headers into a oneway stream struct to avoid repeated string\noperations.\nFake headers a la HTTP/2.\nPointing to the request/response frame numbers, showing the full request\nurl in all associated frames.\nConsolidating some common code with HTTP/2 around similar HTTP\nsemantics.\nHandling implementations where Content-Length is not sent, and also the\nFIN bit is sent in a QUIC STREAM frame that has zero data, so the\nHTTP/3 dissector doesn't get called (and would have to return a bogus\n1 byte to work around #12368, #15159.)\n\nFix #21384 (outside of #21388). Ping #16761. Ping #20610.\n",
